## Fix round 1 — Terminal precedence and replay semantics

### Status and commit

Both round-1 findings are fixed in the same production file. No tests were
added, modified, or run. No provider, Docker daemon, hidden data, push, upload,
or subagent was used.

- Commit: `4b22b45c49a75a51c104303e0044209cc24c13a0`
- Subject: `Harden PIT optimizer terminal replay`
- Committed file: `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py`
- Commit diff: 118 insertions, 11 deletions

### Critical finding correction

Any complete, started call whose authoritative `total_tokens` exceed the exact
authorization reservation now forces `budget_exhausted` before terminal receipt
cross-checking and publication. This precedence is unconditional: it overrides
an outcome-derived `failed` code and an explicitly requested `failed` or
`cancelled` code.

The same precedence is applied by `verify_reconciliation`, so recovery with the
caller's original non-budget terminal request verifies the durably committed
`budget_exhausted` close rather than disagreeing with it.

### Replay-validation correction

Schema-v3 `_read_records` now reconstructs and validates the terminal state
machine while replaying the hash chain:

- each reconciliation is accumulated in lease call order with its exact
  reservation and provider facts;
- any non-accepted reconciliation requires its terminal lease close as the
  immediately following record;
- a terminal audit receipt, when present, fixes the exact permitted close code;
- without a receipt, the replay admits the same explicit
  failed/cancelled/budget terminal choices allowed by the mutation API;
- every resource overage requires an immediate `budget_exhausted` close and an
  overage receipt cannot claim another terminal code;
- `completed` requires the exact full six-call plan, with every call satisfying
  the complete accepted-provider contract;
- `early_stop` requires a non-empty, proper accepted prefix of that exact plan;
- empty, non-accepted, excess, partial-completed, and full-early-stop histories
  are rejected on reopen.

The complete accepted-provider predicate is shared by replay and
`close_run_lease`, eliminating drift between mutation-time and reopen-time
validation.

